}
----end of code block ----
Impact
An attacker could call the function
addPrincipalToCommitmentGroup()
with arguments(ex. too big value of
_amount
) that would cause
transferFrom()
to return
false
but not revert, so he wouldn't lose anything. Then pool shares will be minted to his address, which he can exchange for principal tokens of other users using the
burnSharesToWithdrawEarnings()
function:
----new code block----
function burnSharesToWithdrawEarnings(
 uint256 _amountPoolSharesTokens,
 address _recipient
    ) external returns (uint256) {
        poolSharesToken.burn(msg.sender, _amountPoolSharesTokens);
​
 uint256 principalTokenValueToWithdraw = _valueOfUnderlying(
            _amountPoolSharesTokens,
            sharesExchangeRateInverse()
        );
​
        totalPrincipalTokensWithdrawn += principalTokenValueToWithdraw;
​
        principalToken.transfer(_recipient, principalTokenValueToWithdraw);
​
