Impact
The time-weighted average mechanism, likely intended to incentivize longer-term holding and prevent gaming of the distribution system, is completely ineffective
The contract behavior may not align with the protocol's economic incentive design
Tools Used
Manual Review
Foundry
Recommendations
Modify the reward calculation to properly incorporate the time-weighted average:
----new code block----
function _calculatePendingRewards(address user) internal view returns (uint256) {
 uint256 userVotingPower = veRAACToken.getVotingPower(user);
 if (userVotingPower == 0) return 0;
​
 uint256 totalVotingPower = veRAACToken.getTotalVotingPower();
 if (totalVotingPower == 0) return 0;
​
 // Get the time-weighted average value for the distribution period
 uint256 timeWeightedAverage = distributionPeriod.calculateAverage(block.timestamp);

 // Calculate user's share based on the time-weighted average
 uint256 share = (timeWeightedAverage * userVotingPower) / totalVotingPower;

 return share > userRewards[user] ? share - userRewards[user] : 0;
}
----end of code block ----
Consider adding a minimum claim delay (the mapping already exists in the contract but is not used yet):
----new code block----
function claimRewards(address user) external override nonReentrant whenNotPaused returns (uint256) {
 if (user == address(0)) revert InvalidAddress();

 // Check if enough time has passed since last claim
 // set to an appropriate value
 if (block.timestamp < lastClaimTime[user] + 1 days) {
 revert ClaimTooEarly();
    }
​
 uint256 pendingReward = _calculatePendingRewards(user);
 if (pendingReward == 0) revert InsufficientBalance();
​
 // Reset user rewards before transfer
    userRewards[user] = totalDistributed;

 // Update last claim time
    _updateLastClaimTime(user);
​
 // Transfer rewards
    raacToken.safeTransfer(user, pendingReward);
​
 emit RewardClaimed(user, pendingReward);
 return pendingReward;
}
----end of code block ----

Summary
The function
onAfterRemoveLiquidity
is responsible for handling post-liquidity removal operations, including fee calculations and administering liquidity back to the pool or QuantAMMAdmin. However, there is a subtle miscalculation in the
maxAmountsIn
parameter in line
541
during admin fee processing, particularly when
localData.adminFeePercent
> 0
Vulnerability Details
When the admin fee is calculated and accrued fees are intended to be added back to the liquidity pool, the value of
maxAmountsIn
becomes slightly less than the actual token amounts required. This leads to a mismatch during the
addLiquidity
operation in the
_vault
, which results in a reversion with the following error:
----new code block----
AmountInAboveMax(token, amountInRaw, params.maxAmountsIn[i])
----end of code block ----
This issue prevents liquidity removal operations from completing successfully, effectively locking user funds in the pool.
The problem lies in the following block of code:
----new code block----
 if (localData.adminFeePercent > 0) {
            _vault.addLiquidity(
                AddLiquidityParams({
                    pool: localData.pool,
                    to: IUpdateWeightRunner(_updateWeightRunner).getQuantAMMAdmin(),
                    maxAmountsIn: localData.accruedQuantAMMFees,
                    minBptAmountOut: localData.feeAmount.mulDown(localData.adminFeePercent) / 1e18,
                    kind: AddLiquidityKind.PROPORTIONAL,
                    userData: bytes("")
                })
            );
 emit ExitFeeCharged(
                userAddress,
                localData.pool,
                IERC20(localData.pool),
                localData.feeAmount.mulDown(localData.adminFeePercent) / 1e18
            );
        }
----end of code block ----
Here:
localData.accruedQuantAMMFees[i]
is calculated using:
----new code block----
localData.accruedQuantAMMFees[i] = exitFee.mulDown(localData.adminFeePercent);
----end of code block ----
However, as vault calculation favours the vault over the user, the calculated
amountInRaw
in vault is slightly more than the
params.maxAmountsIn[i]
. Ref-
1
When the
_vault
validates the input via:
----new code block----
if (amountInRaw > params.maxAmountsIn[i]) {
 revert AmountInAboveMax(token, amountInRaw, params.maxAmountsIn[i]);
}
----end of code block ----
The slight discrepancy causes a reversion, even when the difference is only by 1 wei.
