Vulnerability Report: Denial of Service in BorrowingModule.repay
Context
File:
RiskManager.sol#L113
Description
Denial of Service in
BorrowingModule.repay
occurs when the supply cap is reached. In
Borrowing.sol
, the
repay
function is used to clear a user's debt by pulling assets to the contract and decreasing their borrowing balance by the repaid amount.
The issue arises when this function reverts with
E_SupplyCapExceeded
if the borrowing cap is reached, due to a logic issue in the
RiskManagerModule.checkVaultStatus
function. This is triggered by the
initOperation(OP_REPAY, CHECKACCOUNT_NONE)
action during
repay
, which asks the
EthereumVaultConnector
to perform a
requireVaultStatusCheck
on the vault.
The implementation of this critical risk management function incorrectly reverts if the supply grows and exceeds the cap, which can happen during a repayment when close to the vault's cap:
----new code block----
if (supply > vaultCache.supplyCap && supply > prevSupply) revert E_SupplyCapExceeded();
----end of code block ----
An attacker can exploit this vulnerability by depositing tokens up to the supply cap limit, preventing borrowers from repaying their loans and improving their health status. This breaks a critical system invariant:
BM_INVARIANT_P
, which states that a user can always repay their debt in full. By doing so, the attacker can force liquidations, causing significant damage to the overall health of vaults with caps.
Impact
As per Cantina's Severity Criteria:
High Impact:
Funds can be lost.
High:
Denial of Service in repayments poses a threat to the solvency of the protocol as it prevents a debt reduction operation.
Important system invariant is broken.
Likelihood
As per Cantina's Severity Criteria:
High Likelihood:
Any participant can trigger this bug in the protocol.
It will consistently happen when the supply cap exists, a likely scenario especially in guarded launches or new vaults, and can be triggered by an attacker with sufficient funds to saturate the limit.
Proof of Concept
