https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-06-putty/blob/3b6b844bc39e897bd0bbb69897f2deff12dc3893/contracts/src/PuttyV2.sol#L302-L308
https://github.com/code-423n4/2022-06-putty/blob/3b6b844bc39e897bd0bbb69897f2deff12dc3893/contracts/src/PuttyV2Nft.sol#L11-L18
Vulnerability Details
Putty uses ERC721
safeTransfer
and
safeTransferFrom
throughout the codebase to ensure that ERC721 tokens are not transferred to non ERC721 receivers. However, the initial position mint in
fillOrder
uses
_mint
rather than
_safeMint
and does not check that the receiver accepts ERC721 token transfers:
PuttyV2#fillOrder
----new code block----
 // create long/short position for maker
        _mint(order.maker, uint256(orderHash));
​
 // create opposite long/short position for taker
 bytes32 oppositeOrderHash = hashOppositeOrder(order);
        positionId = uint256(oppositeOrderHash);
        _mint(msg.sender, positionId);
----end of code block ----
PuttyV2Nft#_mint
----new code block----
 function _mint(address to, uint256 id) internal override {
 require(to != address(0), "INVALID_RECIPIENT");
 require(_ownerOf[id] == address(0), "ALREADY_MINTED");
​
        _ownerOf[id] = to;
​
 emit Transfer(address(0), to, id);
    }
----end of code block ----
Impact
If a maker or taker are a contract unable to receive ERC721 tokens, their options positions may be locked and nontransferable. If the receiving contract does not provide a mechanism for interacting with Putty, they will be unable to exercise their position or withdraw assets.
Recommendation
Consider implementing the
require
check in Solmate's
ERC721#_safeMint
in your own mint function:
----new code block----
 function _safeMint(address to, uint256 id) internal virtual {
        _mint(to, id);
​
 require(
            to.code.length == 0 ||
                ERC721TokenReceiver(to).onERC721Received(msg.sender, address(0), id, "") ==
                ERC721TokenReceiver.onERC721Received.selector,
 "UNSAFE_RECIPIENT"
        );
    }
----end of code block ----
However, note that calling
_safeMint
introduces a reentrancy opportunity! If you make this change, ensure that the mint is treated as an interaction rather than an effect, and consider adding a reentrancy guard:
----new code block----
 /*  ~~~ EFFECTS ~~~ */
​
 // create opposite long/short position for taker
 bytes32 oppositeOrderHash = hashOppositeOrder(order);
        positionId = uint256(oppositeOrderHash);
​
 // save floorAssetTokenIds if filling a long call order
 if (order.isLong && order.isCall) {
            positionFloorAssetTokenIds[uint256(orderHash)] = floorAssetTokenIds;
        }
​
 // save the long position expiration
        positionExpirations[order.isLong ? uint256(orderHash) : positionId] = block.timestamp + order.duration;
​
 emit FilledOrder(orderHash, floorAssetTokenIds, order);
​
 /* ~~~ INTERACTIONS ~~~ */
​
        _safeMint(order.maker, uint256(orderHash));
        _safeMint(msg.sender, positionId);
----end of code block ----
Alternatively, document the design decision to use
_mint
and the associated risk for end users.
outdoteth (Putty Finance) acknowledged, but disagreed with severity and commented
:
It's unlikely a contract will have all the setup required to interact with PuttyV2 but not be able to handle ERC721 tokens. Adding a check via safeMint adds a gas overhead as well as another re-entrancy attack vector so there is a tradeoff (as noted in the issue report^^).
Report: Contracts that can’t handle ERC721 tokens will lose their Putty ERC721 position tokens.
HickupHH3 (judge) commented
:
In addition, some contracts may have custom logic in their
onERC721Received()
implementation that is triggered only by the safe methods and not their "unsafe" counterparts.

Data Validation: Integer Math Functions
Difficulty
: Medium
Type
: Data Validation
Description
Several math functions for integer values are implemented incorrectly and do not handle overflow cases.
AbsValue Function
The
AbsValue
function allows overflow to occur for minimum integer values and does not panic.
----new code block----
func AbsValue[T Ordered](value T) T {
 if value < 0 {
 return -value // never happens for unsigned types
    }
 return value
}
----end of code block ----
Figure 3.1: The AbsValue function implementation for generic Ordered types (nitro/util/arbmath/math.go#77–83)
SaturatingAdd Function
The
SaturatingAdd
function returns incorrect values when reaching saturation. The positive case returns -1 instead of the expected maximum integer value. This is because the right-shift operator
>>
implements a signed arithmetic shift for integer types. In a similar manner, the negative case returns 0 instead of the minimum integer value.
----new code block----
// SaturatingAdd add two integers without overflow
func SaturatingAdd[T Signed](a, b T) T {
    sum := a + b
 if b > 0 && sum < a {
        sum = ^T(0) >> 1
    }
 if b < 0 && sum > a {
        sum = (^T(0) >> 1) + 1
    }
 return sum
}
----end of code block ----
Figure 3.2: The SaturatingAdd function implementation for generic Integer types (nitro/util/arbmath/math.go#270–280)
SaturatingSub Function
The
SaturatingSub
function does not handle overflow in the subtrahend when it equals the minimum negative integer value. It further compounds the errors from the
SaturatingAdd
function.
----new code block----
// SaturatingSub subtract an int64 from another without overflow
func SaturatingSub(minuend, subtrahend int64) int64 {
 return SaturatingAdd(minuend, -subtrahend)
}
----end of code block ----
Figure 3.3: The SaturatingSub function implementation for generic integer types (nitro/util/arbmath/math.go#291–294)
SaturatingMul Function
The
SaturatingMul
function, similarly to the
SaturatingAdd
function, returns -1 for the positive saturating case and 0 for the negative case.
----new code block----
// SaturatingMul multiply two integers without over/underflow
func SaturatingMul[T Signed](a, b T) T {
    product := a * b
 if b != 0 && product/b != a {
 if (a > 0 && b > 0) || (a < 0 && b < 0) {
            product = ^T(0) >> 1
        } else {
            product = (^T(0) >> 1) + 1
        }
    }
 return product
}
----end of code block ----
Figure 3.4: The SaturatingMul function implementation for generic integer types (nitro/util/arbmath/math.go#313–324)
SaturatingNeg Function
The
SaturatingNeg
function incorrectly checks for the case when the provided value equals -1 (
^T(0)
) instead of the minimum integer value. It further incorrectly returns -1 instead of the maximum value in the special case.
----new code block----
// Negates an int without underflow
func SaturatingNeg[T Signed](value T) T {
 if value == ^T(0) {
 return (^T(0)) >> 1
    }
 return -value
}
----end of code block ----
Figure 3.5: The SaturatingNeg function implementation for generic integer types (nitro/util/arbmath/math.go#368–374)
Usage of Functions
The highlighted math functions are used in a few places throughout the codebase.
For example, the
SaturatingAdd
function is used in
internal_tx.go
.
----new code block----
gasSpent := arbmath.SaturatingAdd(perBatchGas, arbmath.SaturatingCast[int64](batchDataGas))
----end of code block ----
