Recommendations
Short term, consider reverting the changes that allow for generic type implementations of the arbmath functions. Additionally, include unit tests that cover important edge cases. Alternatively (although we do not recommend the usage of the
unsafe
package), implement the correct integer math functions using helper functions
MaxSignedValue
and
MinSignedValue
.
----new code block----
// MaxSignedValue returns the maximum value for a signed integer type T
func MaxSignedValue[T Signed]() T {
 return T(1<<(8*unsafe.Sizeof(T(0))-1) - 1)
}
​
// MinSignedValue returns the minimum value for a signed integer type T
func MinSignedValue[T Signed]() T {
 return T(1 << (8*unsafe.Sizeof(T(0)) - 1))
}
----end of code block ----
Figure 3.13: The MaxSignedValue and MinSignedValue helper functions
Return the maximum integer value in the case of a positive overflow, and the minimum integer value in the case of a negative integer overflow, for the
SaturatingAdd
and
SaturatingMul
functions.
----new code block----
// SaturatingAdd adds two integers without overflow
func SaturatingAdd[T Signed](a, b T) T {
    sum := a + b
 if b > 0 && sum < a {
 return MaxSignedValue[T]()
    }
 if b < 0 && sum > a {
 return MinSignedValue[T]()
    }
 return sum
}
----end of code block ----
