Beacon Kit Deposit Handling Vulnerability
Context
block_builder.go#L304-L326
Description
Deposits in Beacon Kit are never pruned (
pruning.go#L40-L56
) in order to be able to compute the hash tree root when proposing a block to set the
Eth1Data
field in the Beacon block. While this is by design, it presents an important flaw, primarily with the current implementation. Combined with a minimum staking amount of only 1 BERA (
DepositContract.sol#L19-L21
) and the fact that when proposing a block, it will always retrieve all the deposits (
block_builder.go#L305-L307
) (note starting from 0 and the comment make this clear) as follows:
----new code block----
// Grab all previous deposits from genesis up to the current index + max deposits per block.
deposits, err := s.sb.DepositStore().GetDepositsByIndex(
 0, depositIndex+s.chainSpec.MaxDepositsPerBlock(),
)
----end of code block ----
This results in a loop from 0 to the last deposit, stopping only when there are no more deposits or when reaching
endIdx
. Thus, if there are 1 million deposits, it will loop 1 million times. While there are memory resource concerns, the CPU/latency impact seems more critical.
----new code block----
func (kv *KVStore) GetDepositsByIndex(
    startIndex uint64,
    depRange uint64,
) (ctypes.Deposits, error) {
    kv.mu.RLock()
 defer kv.mu.RUnlock()
 var (
        deposits = make(ctypes.Deposits, 0, depRange)
        endIdx   = startIndex + depRange
    )
 for i := startIndex; i < endIdx; i++ {
        deposit, err := kv.store.Get(context.TODO(), i)
 switch {
 case err == nil:
            deposits = append(deposits, deposit)
 case errors.Is(err, sdkcollections.ErrNotFound):
 return deposits, nil
 default:
 return deposits, errors.Wrapf(
                err, "failed to get deposit %d, start: %d, end: %d", i, startIndex, endIdx,
            )
        }
    }
    kv.logger.Debug("GetDepositsByIndex", "start", startIndex, "end", endIdx)
 return deposits, nil
}
----end of code block ----
Such design opens the door for a realistic attack vector: spamming the EVM chain with deposit requests at the minimum deposit amount (assuming 1 BERA is not an expensive amount) will bloat the CL chain over time. The attacker does not lose any money in this scenario, as their tokens are staked normally and earning yield.
Testnet
has an average block rate of 3 seconds, which translates to approximately 28,800 blocks per day.
A maximum of 16 deposits can be processed per block (
base.go#L116
).
Thus, 460,800 deposits could potentially be created every day at a cost of 460,800 BERA. This could lead to the
PrepareProposal
function reaching a breaking point at some stage, halting the chain, as all proposers would timeout on their turn, overflowing the
TimePropose
CometBFT configuration.
Current Flow of Deposits
User deposits 1 BERA against the Deposit contract.
FinalizeBlock
→ Deposits are Enqueued (
finalize_block.go#L97
).
FinalizeBlock
→ Deposits are Pruned (
finalize_block.go#L108
), but they are not actually pruned (by design):
----new code block----
func (s *Service[
    _, _, ConsensusBlockT, _,
    GenesisT, ConsensusSidecarsT,
]) FinalizeBlock(
    ctx sdk.Context,
    req *cmtabci.FinalizeBlockRequest,
) (transition.ValidatorUpdates, error) {
 // STEP 4: Post Finalizations cleanups
 // fetch and store the deposit for the block
    blockNum := blk.GetBody().GetExecutionPayload().GetNumber()
    s.depositFetcher(ctx, blockNum) // <- DEPOSITS Enqueue
 // store the finalized block in the KVStore.
    slot := blk.GetSlot()
 if err = s.storageBackend.BlockStore().Set(blk); err != nil {
        s.logger.Error(
 "failed to store block", "slot", slot, "error", err,
        )
    }
 // prune the availability and deposit store
    err = s.processPruning(blk) // <- DEPOSITS Pruned, but none are actually pruned
 if err != nil {
        s.logger.Error("failed to processPruning", "error", err)
    }
 go s.sendPostBlockFCU(ctx, st, cBlk)
 return valUpdates, nil
}
----end of code block ----
PrepareProposal
→ Deposits from the previous block are read from the store (
block_builder.go#L305-L307
). Here is the issue; as described earlier, at some point when deposits become too large, this will timeout every proposer and halt the chain.
Proof of Concept
Run an end-to-end local network using Kurtosis with the following modifications: process the 1 million fake deposits slowly (5k per block) to ramp up the total deposit quickly and simulate the issue.
Simulate 1 million deposits when the end-to-end deposit test is run (ignoring the deposit from the test, just duplicating the first one encountered in the log).
Increase the maximum deposit to 5000 (otherwise, at 16 deposits per block, it would take hours to reach the inflection point).
Comment out some traces that otherwise would get very noisy.
----new code block----
// Max operations per block constants.
// - MaxDepositsPerBlock: 16,
// + MaxDepositsPerBlock: 5000,
// Grab all previous deposits from genesis up to the current index + max deposits per block.
+ before := time.Now()
deposits, err := s.sb.DepositStore().GetDepositsByIndex(
 0, depositIndex+s.chainSpec.MaxDepositsPerBlock(),
)
if err != nil {
 return err
}
+ spent := time.Since(before)
+ s.logger.Info(
+ "****** (EL) buildBlockBody(): RAW",
+ "num_deposits", uint64(len(deposits)),
+ "spent", spent,
+ )
----end of code block ----
Conclusion
We can clearly see how the chain is slowly bleeding to death, timing out (e.g.,
Timed out module=consensus dur=6500 height=71 round=7 step=RoundStepPropose
), building a block every minute when reaching around 200k deposits. It's anticipated that the chain would halt or produce a block every hour or so. The logic remains consistent despite allowing for 5k deposits to be processed per block, as this merely accelerates the problem by accumulating more deposits overall. As soon as
GetDepositsByIndex
takes around 1500ms (around 150k deposits), the chain begins to degrade drastically.
Recommendation
It is unclear how Ethereum 2.0 handles this, but it is unlikely they follow the same approach. If they do, they have a significant mitigation by requiring 32 ETH per minimum stake, an amount of considerable capital. Today, Ethereum has approximately 1 million validators. If all those were 32 ETH, each propose would involve looping over 1M, which is impractical. Ethereum's Beacon Chain does not have instant finality, which may help; it has a 13-minute finalization period.
To address this issue, each proposal that processes deposits should start hashing from the last deposits and simply continue from the last one, instead of starting from the beginning with each block.
Berachain
Acknowledged. Considering implementation fixes for the future.

Audit Report
Severity: Medium Risk
Context
OrderBook.sol#L790
Description
The
collectFees
function is guarded by an inline access control require statement condition which prevents anyone, except a host, from invoking the function. Only the host of the market is authorized to invoke, effectively delivering all collected fees, including the part of the fees belonging to the DAO.
----new code block----
function collectFees() external nonReentrant {
 require(msg.sender == _host(), Errors.ACCESS); // @audit only host authorized
 if (_baseFeeBalance > 1) {
        _collectFees(_baseToken, _baseFeeBalance - 1);
        _baseFeeBalance = 1;
    }
 if (_quoteFeeBalance > 1) {
        _collectFees(_quoteToken, _quoteFeeBalance - 1);
        _quoteFeeBalance = 1;
    }
}
----end of code block ----
This access control is too strict and can lead to funds being locked permanently in the worst-case scenario. As the host is a single point of failure, if access to the wallet is lost or is incorrectly transferred, the fees for both the host and the DAO will be locked.
Recommendation
It is recommended to remove the access control from the
collectFees
function, as collected fees are transferred to fixed addresses being the host and the treasury. In such a setup, anyone should be able to invoke the function and trigger collected fees delivery at any time, and it should not be limited only to the host of the market.
Clober
Fixed in PR 315.
Spearbit
Verified. Authorization modified. Everyone can trigger the function.

Summary
A critical arithmetic error in the
onAfterRemoveLiquidity
function causes a protocol pool’s “uplift” fee calculation to zero out whenever the pool’s value grows less than 100%. Instead of charging an uplift fee, the code mistakenly reverts to the minimum fee, significantly under-collecting fees for moderate or even substantial positive gains. A proof-of-concept demonstrates that with a 50% price increase, the code applies only the minimum fee (5 BPS) instead of the intended 100 BPS uplift fee, potentially leading to major revenue loss.
Vulnerability Details
In the
UpliftOnlyExample.sol#L474-L476
snippet below, the deposit’s value change
lpTokenDepositValueChange
is computed with integer division:
----new code block----
localData.lpTokenDepositValueChange =
    (int256(localData.lpTokenDepositValueNow) - int256(localData.lpTokenDepositValue)) /
 int256(localData.lpTokenDepositValue);
----end of code block ----
Since Solidity integer division truncates any fractional part, any gain smaller than the deposit’s full value leads to
lpTokenDepositValueChange
being zero. For example, if a position grows 50% (
valueNow = 1.5 × depositValue
), the difference
(1.5D - 1.0D)
is
0.5D
, but integer division by
1.0D
yields zero.
The immediate downstream effect is shown in
UpliftOnlyExample.sol#L478-L490
. The code checks whether
lpTokenDepositValueChange > 0
. If it is zero, the logic incorrectly falls back to the “minimum withdrawal fee,” instead of the intended “uplift fee.”
----new code block----
uint256 feePerLP;
// if the pool has increased in value since the deposit, the fee is calculated based on the deposit value
if (localData.lpTokenDepositValueChange > 0) {
    feePerLP =
        (uint256(localData.lpTokenDepositValueChange) * (uint256(feeDataArray[i].upliftFeeBps) * 1e18)) /
 10000;
}
// if the pool has decreased in value since the deposit, the fee is calculated based on the base value
else {
    feePerLP = (uint256(minWithdrawalFeeBps) * 1e18) / 10000;
}
----end of code block ----
Hence, even when a depositor’s position appreciates by a moderate percentage (e.g., 10%, 50%, or 80%), the computed
lpTokenDepositValueChange
remains
0
and triggers the fallback
minWithdrawalFeeBps
. This drastically undercharges withdrawal fees, effectively nullifying the “uplift” fee logic for positive price changes under 100% growth. As a result, LPs will pay only the minimal fee in scenarios where they would normally be subject to a higher fee based on their real gains.
Impact
This flaw in fee calculation allows LPs to systematically evade the intended uplift fee whenever their position appreciates less than 100%. In real-world conditions, even moderate gains (like 10%, 30%, or 50%) get zeroed out, causing the protocol to charge only the minimum fee instead of a proportionally larger fee based on the actual uplift. As a result, the protocol forfeits significant revenue and violates the intended fee structure:
Serious Undercharging
: The pool cannot collect the correct “uplift” portion, leading to lost revenue that can accumulate quickly over multiple deposits/withdrawals.
Broken Fee Mechanism
: The entire premise of an uplift-based fee becomes unreliable, since nearly any typical growth scenario falls below a factor of 2×, thus never triggering the intended logic.
Exploitable for Profit
: Users aware of this vulnerability can strategically deposit and withdraw under moderate gains to exploit the cheaper fee, amplifying potential losses for the protocol.
Below is a proof-of-concept test demonstrating the incorrect fallback to the minimal fee in a “small positive price change” scenario. The test shows that a 50% increase in pool value still charges only the minimum withdrawal fee instead of the uplift fee:
PoC Test Code
