Fixed in #74 and #101.

Treasury Contract Analysis
The
Treasury.sol
file calculates the
mintAmount
and
redeemAmount
by fetching the price of the LP tokens using
get_virtual_price
. This price may be manipulated temporarily with the help of read-only reentrancy. Since
get_virtual_price
does not have reentrancy protection, manipulation of the price may occur if reentrancy is possible within the curve pool operations.
This has been the root cause of several recent exploits, and it may be useful to mitigate this behavior explicitly as a defense-in-depth measure. Additionally, it may be safer to abort processing if the LP token value decreases, as this represents a violation of a critical invariant. It may make more sense to revert instead of silently accepting the original value in such cases.
Example Code
----new code block----
// Don't allow LP token price to decrease
if (lpTokenPrice < previousLpTokenPrice) {
    lpTokenPrice = previousLpTokenPrice;
} else {
    previousLpTokenPrice = lpTokenPrice;
}
----end of code block ----
Remediation
Before fetching the price in
__getMintAmount
and
__getLpTokenAmount
, call any function that is reentrancy guarded, such as
remove_liquidity
.
Example Code
----new code block----
function __getMintAmount(uint256 _lpTokenAmount) private returns (uint256 mintAmount) {
 uint256[2] calldata amounts;
    ICurve3Pool(token).remove_liquidity(0, amounts);
 uint256 lpTokenPrice = ICurve3Pool(CURVE_3POOL).get_virtual_price();
}
----end of code block ----
----new code block----
function __getLpTokenAmount(uint256 _amount) private returns (uint256 lpTokenAmount) {
 uint256[2] calldata amounts;
    ICurve3Pool(token).remove_liquidity(0, amounts);
 uint256 lpTokenPrice = ICurve3Pool(CURVE_3POOL).get_virtual_price();
}
----end of code block ----
Also consider refactoring the LP token price calculations to:
----new code block----
// Don't allow LP token price to decrease
require(lpTokenPrice >= previousLpTokenPrice);
previousLpTokenPrice = lpTokenPrice;
----end of code block ----
