Context: Pool.sol#L818
Description
Attacker can steal interest by targeting correct ticks which have below prerequisites:
No pending redemption (to prevent attacker funds from getting stuck in redeem).
Current repayment interest is greater than all future repayment interest (this may be true for new/unused ticks).
Once repayment for the loan is being done in a block (when Condition 2 holds true), the attacker can frontrun it with a deposit. The attacker will share up to 50% of interest received. Once repayment completes, the attacker can immediately redeem funds with profit.
Sample Proof of Concept (POC)
----new code block----
it("POC", async function () {
 const val1 = FixedPoint.from("5");
 const val2 = FixedPoint.from("10");
 await pool.connect(accountDepositors[0]).deposit(Tick.encode("5"), val1);
 const borrowTx = await pool
        .connect(accountBorrower)
        .borrow(
            val1,
 30 * 86400,
            nft1.address,
 124,
 FixedPoint.from("6"),
 await sourceLiquidity(FixedPoint.from("5")),
            ethers.utils.solidityPack(["uint16", "uint16", "bytes20"], [3, 20, accountBorrower.address])
        );
 const loanReceipt = (await extractEvent(borrowTx, pool, "LoanOriginated")).args.loanReceipt;
 const decodedLoanReceipt = await loanReceiptLib.decode(loanReceipt);
 await pool.connect(accountDepositors[1]).deposit(Tick.encode("5"), val2);
 const deposit = await pool.deposits(accountDepositors[0].address, Tick.encode("5"));
 const deposit2 = await pool.deposits(accountDepositors[1].address, Tick.encode("5"));
 const totalShares = deposit.shares.add(deposit2.shares);
 const totalValue = decodedLoanReceipt.repayment.add(val2);
 const deposit1Val = (totalValue.mul(deposit.shares).div(totalShares));
 const deposit2Val = (totalValue.mul(deposit2.shares).div(totalShares));
 console.log(deposit2Val);
});
----end of code block ----
Recommendation
This is an edge case which can only happen in very rare scenarios. This is mostly possible for newer and rarely used ticks. Users can be made aware of such risks via documentation before investing in a totally new tick.
MetaStreet
Acknowledged. This is a known design limitation of the deposit price proration scheme in a tick. As the tick is used in multiple loans with overlapping maturities, the opportunity to snipe interest diminishes. Also, sniped interest is not guaranteed as the loan may default.
Sniping 50% of the interest right before repayment of a loan is possible if there's only one loan in that tick. But once there are more loans in that tick, it won’t be as effective.
Further POC
Below is a POC with a similar idea as above, but where a borrower tries to get a "cheaper" loan by doing a flash loan that sandwiches a deposit and his repayment:
Get a huge flash loan of 2000 ETH.
Deposit the 2000 ETH.
Repay his loan.
Withdraw and return his flash loan.
But because there are more than one active loan (3 loans), on net, he is worse off than if he repaid without the flash loan. You can replace the multicall with just repay() in this snippet to see the results without the flash loan:
----new code block----
