If an attacker manages to successfully craft valid payloads using on-chain state to weaponize it, the attack can ultimately lead to critical consequences such as account take-over and arbitrary script execution on victim's browser.
BVSS
AO:A/AC:L/AX:L/C:N/I:N/A:N/D:N/Y:N/R:N/S:C (0.0)
Recommendation
Given that on-chain primitives do not allow for proper sanitization of inputs, and considering the myriad of payloads that can affect web applications, it is recommended to conduct a thorough examination of off-chain components, such as back-end and front-end applications.
Ensure that malicious on-chain payloads are properly sanitized in off-chain premises to prevent payloads from being rendered in a way that could execute arbitrary code in users' browsers.
Remediation Plan
ACKNOWLEDGED:
The
Solayer team
has acknowledged this finding.

Description
Reentrancies on token transfer might trigger the incorrect order of events. The reentrancies require a token with external call capabilities, which is not possible with the current token implementation.
After each token transfer, an event is emitted. If the token transfer is made on a contract with external call capabilities (such as ERC223 or ERC777), a reentrancy might cause events to be emitted in the wrong order:
----new code block----
bool transferred = myIERC20(_tokenAddress).transfer(
    toAddress,
    amount
);
require(transferred, "Transfer failed");
emit Withdrawal(
    toAddress,
    amount,
    withdrawalPermissionRootNonce,
    maxAuthorizedAccountNonce
);
----end of code block ----
Figure 7.1: withdraw (Staking.sol#L251-L263).
----new code block----
bool transferred = myIERC20(_tokenAddress).transfer(
    depositor,
    amount
);
require(transferred, "Transfer failed");
emit PendingDepositRefund(depositor, amount);
----end of code block ----
Figure 7.2: refundPendingDeposit (Staking.sol#L345-L351).
----new code block----
bool transferred = myIERC20(_tokenAddress).transfer(
    toAddress,
    withdrawalAmount
);
require(transferred, "Transfer failed");
emit FallbackWithdrawal(
    toAddress,
    withdrawalAmount
);
----end of code block ----
Figure 7.3: withdrawFallback (Staking.sol#L309-L320).
----new code block----
bool transferred = myIERC20(_tokenAddress).transferFrom(
 msg.sender,
 address(this),
    amount
);
require(transferred, "Transfer failed");
depositNonce = ++_depositNonce;
_nonceToPendingDeposit[depositNonce].depositor = msg.sender;
_nonceToPendingDeposit[depositNonce].amount = amount;
emit Deposit(
 msg.sender,
    amount,
    depositNonce
);
----end of code block ----
Figure 7.4: deposit (Staking.sol#L153-L168).
In addition, the nonce associated with a deposit might not follow the correct order (Figure 7.4).
Exploit Scenario
Staking is deployed with an ERC777 token. Eve uses the reentrancy to trigger incorrect events and confuse the staking off-chain monitor.
Recommendation
Short term, use the
Checks-Effects-Interactions
pattern for all the token interactions (deposit, withdraw, withdrawFallback, refundPendingDeposit).
Long term, continuously run
Slither
on the codebase or use
crytic.io
.

Bug Report: Infinity Pool Advance Reversion
Context
File:
EraBoxcarMidSum.sol#L30
Description
If
tickBin
reaches its max value (equal to
(1 << splits) - 1
), all calls to
Infinity-Pool.advance()
on subsequent blocks will revert due to the check in
EraBoxcarMidSum.sol#L30
. This renders the pool unusable because
infinityPool.advance()
is called at the start of every state-changing function, and there is no way to adjust
tickBin
.
The impact would result in a total Denial of Service (DoS) for the pool, as all functions rely on
advance()
internally. Based on my understanding, this bug could be triggered cheaply on pools that are low in liquidity or empty. Consequently, an upgrade would be necessary to fix the pool because the factory does not allow creating another one for the same token pair/splits. Given that this is a pure griefing vector, I believe medium severity is appropriate.
Proof of Concept
It is important to note that the following Proof of Concept (PoC) consumes an exorbitant amount of gas, which is a separate issue reported in #96. To demonstrate the issue mentioned in the current report, we assume that #96 was fixed and that
infinityPool.swap()
consumes a reasonable amount of gas. Make sure to pass
--gas-limit="18446744073709551615"
to the forge test.
To trigger the issue, call
swap
on an empty pool, bringing
tickBin
to its max value. This action is also feasible on pools with low liquidity.
Steps to Run the Proof of Concept
Copy and paste the following into
test/cantina/poc_max_tick_grief.t.sol
.
Run with:
----new code block----
forge test --match-path 'test/cantina/poc_max_tick_grief.t.sol' --gas-limit="18446744073709551615" -vv .
----end of code block ----
To see the cause of the revert with
-vvvv
, use:
----new code block----
forge test --match-path 'test/cantina/poc_max_tick_grief.t.sol' --gas-limit="18446744073709551615" -vvvv | tail -c 10000
----end of code block ----
----new code block----
